/-! # Second-order neighbourhood, part (b) (`prop:so:kernel`(b)), Jacobson-free

`W = H₃(ℂ_i)` (`standardComplexWindow`), `J₃ = J[t]/(t³)` modelled as triples
`(x₀, x₁, x₂)` (coefficients of `1, t, t²`) with the graded Jordan product
`(x∘y)_k = Σ_{i+j=k} x_i∘y_j` (`j3Ops`); `E₂ = W ⊕ tJ ⊕ t²J` (`E2`, a Jordan subalgebra);
`K = t²W^⊥` (`Kso`), `W^⊥` = zero diagonal and off-diagonal entries in `ℂ_i^⊥`
(`Wperp`; coordinates `re = imI = 0`).

Theorem `E2_jordanHom_kills_K`: every real-linear Jordan homomorphism `f : E₂ → A⁺`, `A` an
associative real algebra (unital or not; `f` unital or not), vanishes on `K`.  Route (no
Jacobson): Shestakov's `ψ` vanishes in `A⁺` (`special_psi_eq_zero`), so `f` kills the three
values `ψ(x, y, t z_r) = t²·12·F₀₂(n_r)` (`n_r = kℓ, iℓ, −k`; computed exactly), `ker f` is
stable under Jordan multiplication by `E₂`, and the 18 coordinate vectors of `K` are
multiples of `w∘P_r` or `w'∘(w∘P_r)` with `w, w'` in a basis of `W` (exact identities). -/

@[expose] public section

namespace AlbertSequential.AppF

